.Dd August 12, 2026
.Dt MONBSD 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm monbsd
.Nd system monitor for FreeBSD laptops
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a lightweight terminal-based system monitor specifically designed for FreeBSD laptops.
It provides granular monitoring of system health, focusing on thermal performance,
power management, and hardware status.
.Pp
The following information is displayed:
.Bl -bullet -offset indent
.It
System uptime, load average, and CPU model.
.It
Real-time CPU and GPU temperatures.
.It
CPU frequency and logical CPU (thread) count.
.It
Memory and swap usage.
.It
Network interface RX/TX rates.
.It
Disk usage for common mount points.
.It
ACPI states, battery status, and fan speed (where supported).
.It
Counts of installed packages and ports.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
The environment is scrubbed with
.Xr clearenv 3
at startup.
The following variables are honored or re-injected:
.Bl -tag -width "SUDO_UID"
.It Ev TERM
The first 64 bytes are validated against the character set
.Va [A-Za-z0-9._+-] .
Values are truncated to 64 characters before being re-injected after
scrubbing; invalid values are discarded.
.It Ev PATH
Reset to a fixed system list
.Pq /bin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/usr/sbin:/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin .
.It Ev SUDO_UID
Consulted to resolve the invoking user's home directory when run via
.Xr sudo 8 .
.El
All other environment variables are cleared.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/dev/cpuctl0" -compact
.It Pa /dev/cpuctl*
Used for direct MSR access to read CPU temperature and performance counters.
.It Pa /dev/io
Used for direct PCI configuration space access.
.El
.Sh PRIVILEGES
For full telemetry,
.Nm
opens
.Pa /dev/cpuctl0
and
.Pa /dev/io
at startup, which requires root; it is typically installed setuid root or run
via
.Xr sudo 8 .
Both descriptors are opened close-on-exec and, if the effective uid is 0,
privileges are permanently dropped with
.Xr setresgid 2
and
.Xr setresuid 2
before the user interface starts; failure to drop is fatal (fail closed).
The descriptors remain usable after the drop because credential checks happen
at
.Xr open 2
time.
.Pp
When run without the ability to open those devices,
.Nm
continues with reduced telemetry: CPU temperature falls back to the ACPI
thermal zone sysctl, live frequency falls back to
.Va dev.cpu.0.freq ,
and the PCI device count is enumerated with
.Xr pciconf 8
instead of direct configuration-space access.
.Pp
MSR-based temperatures, APERF/MPERF live frequency, and direct PCI counting
require the amd64 or i386 architecture; other architectures build and run
with reduced telemetry.
MSR telemetry additionally requires the
.Xr cpuctl 4
module, which can be loaded with
.Dq kldload cpuctl
or via
.Pa /boot/loader.conf .
Without it, and without an ACPI thermal zone, CPU temperature is reported as
.Dq N/A .
.Sh TELEMETRY NOTES
.Bl -dash -offset indent
.It
The
.Dq Ports
figure counts installed packages whose
.Xr pkg 8
repository name is exactly
.Dq local ,
i.e. packages installed from the ports tree outside any configured
repository.
Packages built by tools such as poudriere carry that builder's repository
name and are not counted.
The query is re-run roughly every 60 seconds; a failed query never
overwrites the last known value, which is reported as
.Dq N/A
until a query succeeds.
.It
The PCI device count uses a direct configuration-space scan when
.Pa /dev/io
is open (amd64/i386, privileged), and otherwise falls back to
.Xr pciconf 8 ,
which works for any user on any architecture.
The two enumerations may differ slightly (bridges, hot-plug visibility).
.It
.Xr powerd 8
and powerdxx status are detected by an exact-name process scan via the
.Va kern.proc
sysctl.
When run as a non-root user this requires the default
.Va security.bsd.see_other_uids=1
setting to see root-owned daemons.
.It
On machines without an ACPI battery the battery section reports
.Dq No battery
and the charge bar is hidden.
.El
.Sh INTERACTIVE COMMANDS
The following keys can be used while
.Nm
is running:
.Bl -tag -width "q, Q, Ctrl+C" -compact
.It Ic q, Q, Ctrl+C
Exit the program.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width "128+signo" -compact
.It 0
User quit
.Pq Ic q , Ic Q , No or Ctrl+C .
.It 1
Startup failure (non-terminal stdin/stdout, environment sanitization failure,
or privilege-drop failure).
.It 128+signo
Terminated by a signal; the terminal is restored before exiting.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sysctl 8 ,
.Xr pciconf 8 ,
.Xr pkg 8 ,
.Xr powerd 8 ,
.Xr cpuctl 4
